A recurrence relation is a mathematical equation that describes a sequence of values, each of which is a function of the preceding values. In the context of algorithmic scrutiny, recurrence relations are used to describe the time complexity of algorithms, particularly those that use recursion.

The time complexity of an algorithm is a measure of the amount of computational time required by an algorithm to run to completion. This is often expressed in terms of Big O notation, which describes the upper bound of the time complexity in the worst-case scenario.

Recurrence relations are particularly useful in analyzing the time complexity of recursive algorithms. For example, consider the following recursive algorithm for computing the factorial of a number:

```
function factorial(n) {
    if (n == 0) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return n * factorial(n - 1);
    }
}
```

The time complexity of this algorithm can be described by the recurrence relation T(n) = T(n - 1) + O(1), where T(n) is the time complexity of the algorithm for input size n, and O(1) represents a constant time operation.

To solve this recurrence relation, we can use the method of iteration, which involves expanding the recurrence relation until a pattern emerges. In this case, we get:

